>> I don't know for sure, but AFAIK there never was a C-compiler that
>> used bytes of 7 bit length or longs with 28 bits. But I wouldn't be
>> surprised of compilers with 16 bit longs.
>
> I would. The oldest C manual I can find doesn't even document the 'long' 
> type, but it's explicit that char is 8 bits and int is 16. When long 
> shows up (1978) it's documented to have a minimum magnitude that 
> requires 32 bits to represent. Effectively, the rules have always been 

I was thinking of C compilers for home computer from the 80ies.

They sometimes had quite some special quirks. That at that time there
was no completed ANSI C standard didn't help of course.

NetHacks source code has a lot of crude hacks that work around issues
with those old compilers. For an especially gruesome example -
although in the end "only" a memory problem - look for "horrible
kludge alert" in monst.c.

> that char is at least 8 bits, int is at least 16 and (when the modifiers 
> became available) short ints were at least 16 bits but no larger than 
> (plain) int, and long ints were at least 32 bits but no smaller than int.
>
> A 16-bit long was never acceptable.

Depends on the application. The CCS C Compiler, a compiler for PIC
microcontrollers, defaults short to 1 bit, char and int to 8 and long
to 16 bit. 
http://www.ccsinfo.com/downloads/ccs_c_manual.pdf (Page 37)

This certainly violates ANSI C as those datatypes don't cover the
required minimum ranges of values. I especially can't think of a good
reason why short is 1 bit.

